{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"73651a459f29dd7450c6c7cc39df246c43793a49"},"timestamp":1536749909,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMDYwNjUzNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-420606534"},"message":"[GitMate.io](https://gitmate.io) thinks possibly related issues are https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2303 (Info on metadata standards), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/719 (CRCNS meta-data), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/717 (BALSA meta-data), https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/618 (Embrace meta data), and https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2059 (Support GigaDB as a data provider).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1536752545,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMDYxNzc2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-420617760"},"message":"Hey @vsoch \nDataLad tries to be agnostic re the metadata you are putting in its datasets. Here are the basic principles:\n\n- any metadata source needs to have an *extractor* implementation\n  - extractors read metadata from whatever it is stored in, in whatever format and yield python datatypes    (arbitrary structure, incl. nesting) that can be serialized as JSON\n  - extractors can report on two entities: a dataset as a whole, a file (within these units anything goes)\n  - extractors can (optionally) also report a vocabulary context (turning JSON into JSON-LD), such a context can identify schema.org as the vocabulary (and possibly extend it)\n  - see here for a few examples implementations: https://github.com/datalad/datalad/tree/master/datalad/metadata/extractors\n\n- extractors/metadata sources need to be enabled in a dataset's configuration\n\n- when enabled, an extractor is invoked during metadata aggregation (command `aggregate-metadata`). This lifts the metadata stored in its native form into a representation that DataLad uses internally. This extracted metadata can be placed in superdatasets to enable metadata access for an absent subdataset\n\n- the `metadata` command provides an API to make DataLad report the extracted metadata back to a user (individual pieces or a full dump). The original dataset does not need to be present for this (see aggregation)\n\n- DataLad aims to fulfill the following promises\n  - whatever an extractor produces will be reported via the `metadata` command, there should be no limits on size or structure (within reason, I hope)\n  - multiple metadata sources will not interact with each other, but leave side-by-side in DataLad's internal representation\n  - if it can be JSON-serialized it should work\n\nIn conclusion, you should be completely free to do whatever you think is best within these limits. I'd consider anything that breaks a bug.\n\nNotes: \n\nBeyond being able to \"put anything in\" the metadata, structure matters a lot, if you care about the size of the software stack that is needed to be able to do anything with these data. In https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2753 we are exploring a useful (nested) structure with a manageable JSON-LD context that could be processed by a query tool that is only able to process tables (not graphs). That may or may not be an issue in your context, but is worth thinking about.\n\nATM the semantic aspects of working with DataLad's metadata are sketchy (as in it is hard and impractical to work with metadata of an unknown structure and meaning). That is because the existing extractors produce oversimplified metadata without the necessary markup, and by extension DataLad's own query support does not use this information.\n\nHowever, it sounds like you would not be affected by that, as you are interested in using proper semantic markup, and you want to feed Google's tools with it.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536761039,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMDY2MDM1MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-420660350"},"message":"Besides metadata aspect (even though it might be the main one in your discussion and outlined above by @mih), it would indeed be nice if datalad could interact seamlessly with data archives for data transfer in/out.  The question there would be also how much flexibility you have on your side and what you want to accomplish -- to provide a \n- \"data publication\" platform (similar to zenodo, figshare - only released versions) or\n- \"data hosting\" (OSF etc - be a part of the research/data exchange process even before \"releases\").\n\nFWIW, as you might know, we were in touch with some nice folks from Stanford library (from sdr-support group) while preparing a basic [crawler pipeline](https://github.com/datalad/datalad-crawler/pull/17) to provide access to the datasets from the library.  With that you can\n```shell\n$\u003e datalad create 2012-Mark-Plates\n[INFO   ] Creating a new annex repo at /mnt/btrfs/datasets/datalad/crawl-misc/stanford/2012-Mark-Plates \ncreate(ok): /mnt/btrfs/datasets/datalad/crawl-misc/stanford/2012-Mark-Plates (dataset)\n\n$\u003e cd 2012-Mark-Plates\n\n$\u003e datalad crawl-init --save --template=stanford_lib doc_id=jn023kf3320       \n[INFO   ] Creating a pipeline to crawl data files from None \n[INFO   ] Initiating special remote datalad-archives \n\n$\u003e datalad crawl\n...  # still waiting for downloads to finish. I already complained about slow speeds\n...  # but I think it remains the issue. Try e.g. on \n...  # https://stacks.stanford.edu/file/druid:qd938sk1220/neurovault_snapshot_2016_05_16.tar\n...   # on which I am getting only 800-900 kBps\n```\nand here you go -- you have git/git-annex repository which would access data from the Stanford library, while if desired publishing to github etc.  Outstanding questions are if we were to crawl the entire library:\n  - how to organize datasets - how to name, what possible hierarchy if any\n  - and then the question of metadata extraction @mih was talking about, so people could easily find\n\nBut that is only for \"consumption\" of datasets people \"publish\" there.  Even it is even only a \"data publication\" platform, ideally there should also be the way to upload datasets there from datalad itself.  One of the possible solutions is a custom exporter like we did for figshare: [export-to-figshare](http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/generated/man/datalad-export-to-figshare.html) which prepares an archive (Figshare doesn't support subdirectories) of the entire dataset, uploads it to figshare, and backreferences within git-annex so later people could fetch those files via datalad.  Cons: it is a tarball, so to access a subset of files, full tarball needs to be obtained.\n\nAn ideal \"data publishing/hosting\" should allow both push/pull (download/publish) for any of the files while retaining original directory structure.  Then interactions could be established back at the level of git-annex itself, like it does e.g. for S3 now.  And if download of a full or subset is needed - would generate (reproducibly, so sorting the files and injecting consistent archive header) a zipball on the fly.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3286e9a6aeeb25e1bf3fcf613be9da3d3270e55d"},"timestamp":1536843624,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMDk5Nzc5MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-420997790"},"message":"This is hugely helpful! Here is what I understand so far:\n\n - Datalad is a wrapper of sorts around git-annex - and git-annex is a tool to basically add everything to git minus the files. Meaning I can have version control of metadata for very large datasets. It also means I could have git-annex (controlled via datalad if I like) alongside a traditional git repo.\n - This means that datalad could get deprecated, and the repos I have would still be usable with git-annex (and other tools supporting it)\n\n## Install\nWhen I do datalad search, or datalad install, I'm interacting with datasets [here](http://datasets.datalad.org/) given that I don't specify a URI (e..g., `install //\u003cdataset-name\u003e` and it appears that this \"superdataset\" is just a github repository, --\u003e https://github.com/datalad/datasets.datalad.org is that correct?  \n - What happens when this single repo gets very large? Is the assumption, for example, that these superdatasets should be organized on the level of groups with specific permissions (e.g., one per lab at Stanford and not one for the entire of Stanford) and then compared or stated to be similar by way of collections? (e.g., Lab A has superdataset turkeys-laba, Lab B has superdataset Lab B, both are under collection \"turkeys.\" I ask because I see a folder called \"labs\" And a follow up ...\n - What determines the naming of the superdataset? The datasets within?\n - How do I make a superdataset and interact with it?\n - How do I change some default location from /home/vanessa/datalad? If I create it there, does this mean that I cannot install another superdataset without overriding that one?\n - Just as a note, it doesn't give good instructions for installing git-annex. I'm linked to the git-annex page (if I read the docs, period) and then the traditional install page isn't easy to find, I search on Google, I'm taken to it and then install with a package manager, but I still get an error that the version is too old and to \"use neurodebian\" - I would guess many users don't know what that means.\n\n## Workflow\nOkay so now I'm going to pretend to use it for my use case - moving files from some local place to Google Cloud Storage and then archive. I'd start with some folder of data.\n\n 1. First I'd ensure that it conforms to an organizational standard by way of a validator. \n - Does datalad have validators for structure, or rely on third party (e.g.., BIDS validator). I would want to have a general definition of the structure in a file and then run it over the directories starting at the root, and tell the user what's not right.\n 2. When I'm validated, I would want datalad to install a dataset there. The level of dataset can be determined based on my use case. Let's say it's just one big folder with subfolders, each subfolder corresponding to one subject in my study (de-identified).\n 3. Adding the datasets to the folder, period, I can commit in the same way with Github. But the validation is up to me? I am able to add a metadata template of sorts, is that checked against the data or just \"I trust you\" sort of deal? These seem to be the [extractors](https://github.com/datalad/datalad/tree/master/datalad/metadata/extractors). And they tell datalad how to parse files in a dataset, but not ensure they follow the format. What if I need multiple extractors within a single dataset?\n 4. Assuming I've added data and have metadata defined, I then want to get it onto Google Cloud. There doesn't seem to be a \"put\" action - is there an integration with Google Cloud Storage to \"put\" data there? Based on a version? I would then want to be able to do the equivalent \"datalad get \u003cgc://\u003e for that version - this is what the library would do, get the metadata (and organizational) and create an archive on their server.\n\nThanks for answering my questions! Apologies for the noobness, I'm still reading a lot and getting the hang of the bigger ideas.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536844746,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTAwMzgwNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-421003807"},"message":"uff... what a nice list of questions/concerns!  I only wonder how should we proceed, since\n- replying here is difficult to associate/inline with the questions\n- it will grow too long/hard to manage too quick\nI wonder if we should try doing it on https://neurostars.org so each question is a separate subthread?  or may be you see a better way? (e.g. a google doc)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1536847200,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTAxNzU0Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-421017547"},"message":"@yarikoptic +1 for neurostars or something equally public (not gdocs, please)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3286e9a6aeeb25e1bf3fcf613be9da3d3270e55d"},"timestamp":1536850318,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTAzNTU3Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-421035577"},"message":"haha ok, you got it! I'm going for a quick run before the storm comes in, but I'll be on this later today. +1 for a location where the questions can be made modular and preserved until the end of time... or until the database blows up @brainstorm :wink:","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1536850843,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTAzODgxNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-421038814"},"message":"@vsoch would you be so kind to repost it on neurostars with each question into a separate subthread?  I promise to reply promptly ;-)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"f390b251abce5d0bbad2f4ae4ef4dd02694ff293"},"timestamp":1536861382,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQyMTA5NjIxMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2814#issuecomment-421096213"},"message":"@vsoch you mean @INCF blowing the neurostars database up since I wasn't working there anymore and warned them **repeatedly** that they should have taken care of it and make snapshots and proper AWS backups? ;)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"3286e9a6aeeb25e1bf3fcf613be9da3d3270e55d"},"timestamp":1536862885,"metadat